Walk the list once and keep a set of the numbers seen so far. For each number, check whether target minus that number is already in the set; set lookups are O(1) on average. That is O(n) time for O(n) extra space, the classic trade of memory for speed.

def has_pair(nums, target):
    seen = set()
    for n in nums:
        if target - n in seen:
            return True
        seen.add(n)
    return False

Put the base case first: when exp is 0, return 1. Reject negative exponents explicitly. In real code, use the built-in pow or the ** operator, which already does this.

def power(base, exp):
    if exp < 0:
        raise ValueError("exp must be non-negative")
    if exp == 0:
        return 1
    half = power(base, exp // 2)
    if exp % 2 == 0:
        return half * half
    return half * half * base

